{
  "index": 77,
  "text": "Based on the \u201cin situ tumor vaccine\u201d concept, SABR plus ICI has been widely explored in advanced solid tumors. Although the clinical benefit of ISABR has been  in NSCLC, the reported outcomes show considerable heterogeneity across studies 10, 41, 42. This heterogeneity is largely driven by inter- and intratumoral genomic diversity, which generates distinct immune microenvironments in primary and metastatic sites 43, 44, limiting systemic immune activation and consistent abscopal effects 45. To address this problem, some clinicians have proposed irradiating multiple lesions rather than relying on single-site SABR 46. However, that approach is often limited by technical challenges, including the inability to deliver ablative doses to large tumors and to tumors that are located close to critical structures 47. The local efficacy of SABR in this trial may also reflect the intrinsic potency of ablative doses rather than immune effects alone. Accordingly, LDRT has emerged as a practical complementary strategy to remodel the TIME 20, 48, 49, enhance immune cell infiltration, and potentiate antitumor immunity 48, 50.",
  "style": "Normal",
  "is_heading": false
}

{
  "index": 78,
  "text": "Given the complementary immunomodulatory effects of SABR and LDRT, the triple combination of SABR + LDRT + ICI is biologically rational 21, 51, 52. A phase Ib study evaluated a chemotherapy-free regimen that combines SABR, LDRT, and sintilimab for the treatment of PD-L1-positive stage IV NSCLC and revealed acceptable safety and evidence of LDRT-induced immune modulation 23. However, its median PFS of 8.6 months did not exceed historical benchmarks for standard ICI or chemoimmunotherapy 53, 54. By contrast, our study used standard first-line chemoimmunotherapy regardless of PD-L1 status. Although PD-L1-high tumors may derive greater benefit from single-agent ICI, chemotherapy-free strategies remain restricted to patients with PD-L1 \u226550% 55, 56. Importantly, our results revealed no significant PFS or OS advantage in PD-L1-high patients compared with PD-L1-low/negative patients. IHBRT triggers broad immune activation and reshapes the tumor immune landscape, with efficacy observed across PD-L1 subgroups. This is consistent with trials suggesting radiotherapy may benefit PD-L1-negative/low NSCLC 10, 57, 58, supporting its potential broader applicability. We acknowledge that the lack of posttreatment biopsies prevents us from evaluating dynamic PD-L1 alterations during treatment. Additionally, no significant difference in PFS and OS was observed between squamous and nonsquamous subgroups in this study, despite prior reports suggesting squamous NSCLC

{
  "index": 79,
  "text": "The results of our exploratory clinical study demonstrated that compared with preradiotherapy levels, the proportions of cyto-CD8+ T cells and cyto-NK cells increased significantly after IHBRT. We further explored the underlying mechanisms using a murine metastatic NSCLC model. Previous studies have shown that LDRT combined with immunotherapy can convert the TIME into one characterized by extensive infiltration by CD8+ T cells, particularly exhausted CD8+ T (CD8+ Tex) cells 20, 62, 63. LDRT combined with anti-PD-1 treatment prolonged OS in the NSCLC metastatic model, although median OS did not differ statistically between the Control and anti-PD-1 groups. This may be due to the limited release of tumor antigens 64, which would impair effective tumor control. By contrast, IHBRT induced a \u201chot\u201d immune phenotype in LDRT lesions and enriched functionally activated effector CD8+ T cells via the Cxcl10\u2013Cxcr3 axis, rather than exhausted T cells. Notably, ISABR alone induced abscopal effects in lung metastases, which were substantially augmented by adding LDRT to metastatic sites. The robust serum cytokine elevation induced by IHBRT, but not by ISABR alone, aligns with the enhanced systemic antitumor responses observed in contralateral tumors, supporting that LDRT amplifies systemic immunity beyond SABR alone. These results indicate that SABR-mediated sustained antigen release synergizes with LDRT-mediated immune priming to generate robust s

{
  "index": 80,
  "text": "Moreover, our in vivo studies demonstrated that IHBRT up-regulated NKG2D on NK cells and NKG2DL on tumor cells, enhancing NK cell cytotoxicity by modulating DNA replication and cell cycle progression. These findings are consistent with prior reports that radiation up-regulates NKG2DL in tumor cells, thereby potentiating NKG2D-targeted immunotherapy 65. Importantly, our results delineate a spatial division of labor between CD8+ T cells and NK cells under IHBRT: Cxcr3-mediated CD8+ T cells contribute to primary tumor control, whereas NKG2D-mediated NK cells contribute to the regression of metastatic lesions, with coordinated support from Cxcl10\u2013Cxcr3-mediated CD8+ T cells. This immune effector program enables IHBRT to exert robust local control and coordinated systemic activity against distant metastases.",
  "style": "Normal",
  "is_heading": false
}

{
  "index": 81,
  "text": "In addition to direct effector activation, our in vivo findings showed that IHBRT remodeled the suppressive myeloid compartment by suppressing Spp1+ macrophages and disrupting Spp1-dependent crosstalk with fibroblasts. Given the role of Spp1+ macrophages in promoting immunosuppressive fibroblast barriers 33\u201335, these effects likely contribute to the dismantling of these barriers. Together, these findings provide a novel mechanistic foundation for radiotherapy\u2013immunotherapy synergy and fill a critical mechanistic gap for combined SABR + LDRT strategies.",
  "style": "Normal",
  "is_heading": false
}

{
  "index": 82,
  "text": "While our study provides promising mechanistic insights and demonstrates therapeutic potential, it has some limitations that should be noted. First, the single-arm design and modest sample size may limit generalizability. Second, the scarcity of posttreatment biopsy specimens limited our ability to comprehensively assess dynamic changes in immune cell subsets. Third, although no significant differences in clinical outcomes were observed among patients who received different ICIs in this study, heterogeneous ICI selection without protocol standardization introduces confounding variables. In addition, in our radiotherapy protocol, the general framework combines LDRT with SABR. However, specific dosing regimens remain individualized across patients. Subgroup analyses by metastatic burden, SABR coverage, and liver metastasis status, together with an analysis excluding patients downstaged to stage III, yielded broadly consistent findings. Nevertheless, future randomized controlled trials with expanded cohorts are needed to standardize the radiotherapy protocol and minimize bias. Finally, although murine models offer valuable preclinical insights, they incompletely mimic the complexity of human TIMEs. Findings from these preclinical models therefore require validation in future clinical studies.",
  "style": "Normal",
  "is_heading": false
}

{
  "index": 83,
  "text": "Building on the compelling clinical and mechanistic evidence for IHBRT in driver-negative metastatic NSCLC presented in this study, we suggest that multicenter randomized phase III trials represent the critical next step in definitively establishing both the therapeutic efficacy and the safety profile of this combined approach.",
  "style": "Normal",
  "is_heading": false
}

{
  "index": 85,
  "text": "In conclusion, our study demonstrates that IHBRT yields an encouraging PFS and a manageable safety profile in patients with driver-negative metastatic NSCLC. Mechanistically, our in vivo studies revealed that IHBRT could induce a functional division of labor: Cxcr3-mediated CD8+ T cells dominate primary tumor control, while NKG2D-mediated NK cells and Cxcr3-mediated CD8+ T cells contribute to metastatic lesion regression. Concurrently, IHBRT suppresses Spp1+ macrophages and disrupts their crosstalk with fibroblasts remodeling the stromal microenvironment to facilitate immune infiltration. These coordinated mechanisms provide a scientific basis for combining SABR and LDRT to amplify systemic antitumor immunity.",
  "style": "Normal",
  "is_heading": false
}

